Shearing shock (3D)
-------------------

The test of section 5.3 of Cullen & Dehnen (2010): two uniform streams
collide head-on at Mach 1 (v_x = +-dv with dv = sound speed) in a periodic
box, with a strong sinusoidal shear v_y = s sin(2 pi x / L), s = 100 dv,
superposed. The shear rate at the shock plane exceeds the compression rate by
two orders of magnitude, so any viscosity switch or limiter that reads the
shear as "no shock" fails this test by letting the streams interpenetrate.

The exact solution is the 1D Riemann problem of the collision (two shocks
moving out from x = L/2) and of the receding streams at the box edge (two
rarefactions), while every particle keeps its initial v_y exactly (there is
no pressure gradient along y). plotSolution.py compares the density, v_x and
pressure profiles with that solution and reports the mean change of v_y per
particle in units of s, a measure of the shear momentum diffused by the
viscosity.

The box is made of N glass cubes along x (default 4, glassCube_32, 131k
particles). The code needs to be compiled in 3D (the default).

Note on the shear amplitude: with the paper's s = 100 dv, the few per cent
of the shear momentum that any SPH viscosity diffuses carry an energy of
order (0.03 s)^2, i.e. 10 to 100 times the thermal energy of the gas, so the
box heats and blows apart in every scheme and the Riemann solution is lost.
The default here is s = 10 dv (makeIC.py -s sets it): the shear rate at the
shock still exceeds the compression rate, and the heating stays at the per
cent level.
